#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11fb303b-053a-56d1-9b96-acd75c748b51" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "cd73b214-d887-5dbb-93f9-b71e16711a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-6304" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3)$.\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=3\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=2\\,\\mathbf{i}+3\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= 3j + t[(2i + 3j) - 3j] \\\\\n&= 3j + t(2i) \\\\\n&= 2ti + 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nThus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3)j \\\\\n&= 2i \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = e^{x+y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= e^{2t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)e^{x + y}ds &= ∫_{0}^{1}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}e^{2t + 3} \\cdot 2 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{1}e^{2t + 3}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=2t+3\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2∫_{0}^{1}e^{2t + 3}dt &= ∫_{3}^{5}e^{u}du \\\\\n&= e^{u} \\mid _{3}^{5} \\\\\n&= e^{5} - e^{3} \\\\\n&= e^{3}(e^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "b7d08596-fbe4-5601-a58e-dac90715f7e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9486" :question/problem "Find a definite integral that is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, traversed along the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= i + t[(i + j) - i] \\\\\n&= i + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nThus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(1)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= 0i + j \\\\\n&= j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 1 + \\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{1}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(1 + \\sin t) \\cdot 1 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(1 + \\sin t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14ab317b-fd33-5d6e-9d54-87e749b78283" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Along Segments in Three Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "17ec17a0-11d7-57c1-9e7f-6893ab9e51e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9487" :question/problem "Find the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z$, traversed along the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2)$?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2)$.\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= (j - 3k) + t[(-i + 2j - 2k) - (j - 3k)] \\\\\n&= (j - 3k) + t(-i + j + k) \\\\\n&=-ti + (t + 1)j + (t - 3)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nThus, we have that\n\n$$\nx=-t, y=t+1\n$$\n\nand $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(u) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j + \\frac{dz}{dt}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(-t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t + 1)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t - 3)k \\\\\n&=-i + j + k \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dz}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2+y-z\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (-t)^{2} + (t + 1) - (t - 3) \\\\\n&= t^{2} + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{1}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} + 4) \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} + 4)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813905e0-6397-5f4a-9852-f2786354e977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} + t - 2)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177473} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e87fb3-3758-597d-ad8f-bc243435c51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} - t + 2)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177474} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b887f2-7196-5e96-b12e-911a2b944d81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} - t + 2)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177475} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb86283-de94-5794-b4e9-cd205c139c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}∫_{0}^{1}(9t^{2} + t - 2)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177476} {:answer/id #uuid "7e30222b-eaf5-56d8-9a5d-0de8daf7af73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10∫_{0}^{1}(9t^{2} + t - 2)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcb86283-de94-5794-b4e9-cd205c139c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}∫_{0}^{1}(9t^{2} + t - 2)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177476} :answer-field/id #uuid "112e88bf-ff88-55f7-aa04-e150ad8e079c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177472}] :question/id #uuid "09a46495-b2d7-513a-938f-504451202710" :question/math-academy-id "q-123127" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - y$, traversed along the line segment from $(0, 2)$ to $(3, 1)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e7232be-d370-57da-9b01-40ce80117b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}\\ln (4 - t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177503} {:answer/id #uuid "b6063b9e-f96d-5160-933c-52afe53d2a3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{1}\\ln (3 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177504} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f07303-ca2e-5347-bed4-775205e71c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{1}\\ln (4 - t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177505} {:answer/id #uuid "c81a1b7e-9d43-5494-8241-dd0e9be17a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}\\ln (3 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177506} {:answer/id #uuid "02d39b02-270d-58a2-ac67-423ab56866e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}\\ln (2 - t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7232be-d370-57da-9b01-40ce80117b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}\\ln (4 - t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177503} :answer-field/id #uuid "780bb928-82c4-5468-8a94-5c33fd41a2d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177502}] :question/id #uuid "9fb44c65-3cac-5280-b910-d92f6181f8ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-123269" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = \\ln (x + y)$, traversed along the line segment from $(1, 3)$ to $(0, 3)$?"}]}
